"Detecting Text Manipulation in Images using Vision Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10278v1)\nciteturn29776view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view0\",\"lineno\":80}); Total lines: 246\nL75: These datasets offer limited manipulation diversity and are primarily used for evaluation rather than large-scale training. FakeShield [cite46†Xu et al.(2024)Xu, Zhang, Li, Tang, Huang, and Zhang ] and SIDA [cite47†Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Hu, Li, He, Zhao, Peng, Wu, Huang, and Cheng ] introduce large-scale AI-generated image datasets (based on GANs or diffusion models) for training. However, they lack sufficient evaluation of text-based manipulations.\nL76: Open-Set Text Forensics (OSTF) [cite36†Qu et al.(2025)Qu, Zhong, Guo, and Jin ] addresses this by introducing a dataset with text edits generated using both CNN and diffusion models. We also study ID card datasets to better understand detection performance on the real-world document tampering. PAD [cite56†Tapia et al.(2024)Tapia, Damer, Busch, Espin, Barrachina, Rocamora, Ocvirk, Alessio, Batagelj, Patwardhan, et al. ] propose ID card datasets for presentation attack detection scenarios.\nL77: In contrast, we target digital manipulation reflecting an injection attack scenario. While PAD [cite56†Tapia et al.(2024)Tapia, Damer, Busch, Espin, Barrachina, Rocamora, Ocvirk, Alessio, Batagelj, Patwardhan, et al. ] involves manipulation of original government IDs, which is legally prohibited, FantasyID [cite37†Korshunov et al.(2025)Korshunov, Mohammadi, Vidit, Ecabert, and Marcel ] avoids legal issues by creating fantasy templates and using images with appropriate licenses.\nL78: In this work, we evaluate detection methods on both the OSTF dataset and FantasyID.\nL79: cite57†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: FantasyID [cite37†Korshunov et al.(2025)Korshunov, Mohammadi, Vidit, Ecabert, and Marcel ] and OSTF [cite36†Qu et al.(2025)Qu, Zhong, Guo, and Jin ] Two different templates from our proposed, FantasyID (left) and OSTF (right) dataset. The red box show the manipulated regions and their corresponding zoomed-in image. (Left) Altered text mostly show jagged edges due to inconsistent blending with the background.\nL80: (Right) Zoomed-in view of the tampered region where some of the manipulation artifacts are visible. Such regions occupy small area of the full image making it a challenging detection task.\nL81: #### Image Manipulation Detection.\nL82: Various works [cite38†Guillaro et al.(2023)Guillaro, Cozzolino, Sud, Dufour, and Verdoliva , cite39†Li and Huang(2019) , cite40†Salloum et al.(2018)Salloum, Ren, and Kuo , cite41†Li and Zhou(2018) , cite42†Kwon et al.(2021)Kwon, Yu, Nam, and Lee , cite43†Chen et al.(2021)Chen, Dong, Ji, Cao, and Li , cite44†Ying et al.(2023)Ying, Zhou, Qian, Li, and Zhang , cite45†Yu et al.(2023)Yu, Zhang, Xu, and Zhang ] have proposed detection methods targeting different types of forgeries, such as inpainting, copy-move, and splicing.\nL83: Detection tasks can involve image-level classification or localization of tampered regions. Typically, training datasets contain pristine and manipulated images, with or without annotations indicating the manipulated areas. These manipulations are introduced using CNN-based generative methods or diffusion-based approaches.\nL84: TruFor [cite38†Guillaro et al.(2023)Guillaro, Cozzolino, Sud, Dufour, and Verdoliva ] learns camera-specific noise patterns and identifies tampered regions by detecting inconsistencies in these patterns. HiFi-Net [cite58†Guo et al.(2023)Guo, Liu, Ren, Grosz, Masi, and Liu ] detects image alterations using multi-branch feature extraction.\nL85: MVSS-Net [cite59†Dong et al.(2022)Dong, Chen, Hu, Cao, and Li ] utilizes multi-scale, multi-branch features to better capture image noise, while IML-ViT [cite60†Ma et al.(2023)Ma, Du, Jiang, Hammadi, and Zhou ] employs a transformer-based architecture with edge-guided loss to enhance focus on tampering artifacts. EditGuard [cite61†Zhang et al.(2024)Zhang, Li, Yu, Xu, Li, and Zhang ] embeds watermarks into images and later verifies authenticity based on their presence.\nL86: These methods are trained on specific types of tampering and aim to generalize to unseen manipulation techniques. However, as generative models improve, generalizing to novel manipulations becomes increasingly challenging.\nL87: Text tampering detection methods [cite62†Wang et al.(2022)Wang, Xie, Xing, Wang, Zhu, and Zhang , cite63†Qu et al.(2023)Qu, Liu, Liu, Chen, Peng, Guo, and Jin , cite36†Qu et al.(2025)Qu, Zhong, Guo, and Jin ] highlight the importance of benchmarks tailored to text manipulation, as such edits often affect small image regions. Nevertheless, similar to prior approaches, these methods also struggle to generalize across new editing techniques.\nL88: #### Vision-Language Models for Detection.\nL89: The use of large Vision-Language Models (VLMs) has grown rapidly, largely due to their zero-shot capabilities. Trained on large-scale internet datasets, these models can quickly adapt to downstream applications.\nL90: While proprietary models such as GPT-4o^{2}^{2} 2 cite64†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models/gpt-4o†platform.openai.com have demonstrated strong performance on a range of benchmarks [cite65†Chiang et al.(2024)Chiang, Zheng, Sheng, Angelopoulos, Li, Li, Zhu, Zhang, Jordan, Gonzalez, et al. ], open-source alternatives like Qwen-VL [cite66†Bai et al.(2025)Bai, Chen, Liu, Wang, Ge, Song, Dang, Wang, Wang, Tang, Zhong, Zhu, Yang, Li, Wan, Wang, Ding, Fu, Xu, Ye, Zhang, Xie, Cheng, Zhang, Yang, Xu, and Lin ] and Llama-Vision [cite67†Grattafiori et al.(2024)Grattafiori, Dubey, Jauhri, Pandey, Kadian, Al-Dahle, Letman, Mathur, Schelten, Vaughan, et al. ] are closing the gap.\nL91: These transformer-based models tokenize images into patch sequences and project them into a shared embedding space with text features, enabling rich cross-modal representations.\nL92: Recently, VLMs have been applied to detect and localize image manipulations with FakeShield [cite46†Xu et al.(2024)Xu, Zhang, Li, Tang, Huang, and Zhang ] and SIDA [cite47†Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Hu, Li, He, Zhao, Peng, Wu, Huang, and Cheng ], fine-tuned LLaVA [cite68†Liu et al.(2023)Liu, Li, Wu, and Lee ] models, not only achieving state-of-the-art performance but also generating textual justifications for their predictions, enhancing interpretability.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNotebook for the Touché Lab at CLEF 2025 (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.14256v1)\nciteturn29776view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view2\",\"lineno\":410}); Total lines: 533\nL385: (2023)】 for its disentangled attention and relative positional encoding mechanisms. In parallel, we implement a CrossEncoder based on all-mpnet-base-v2 cite61†Song et al. (2020) , operating solely on the response text, mirroring deployment scenarios where the user query is unavailable. This model is optimized for F1-score and achieves strong performance without requiring architectural complexity or external signals.\nL386: ### 3.2 Methodology\nL387: \nL388: This section presents a complete overview of the classification pipeline for advertisement detection, consolidating data preprocessing, prompt formulation, model training, and evaluation. The approach is designed to align with transformer pretraining objectives and maximize classification performance under limited supervision.\nL389: \nL390: #### 3.2.1 Approach 1\nL391: ##### Task Formulation:\nL392: \nL393: The task is framed as a binary classification problem. Given a system-generated response—and optionally its query—the model must decide whether it contains an advertisement. The model outputs a single label: 1 for promotional content and 0 for neutral responses. We employ a CrossEncoder to leverage token-level interactions that highlight subtle persuasive wording.\nL394: ##### Input Construction and Preprocessing:\nL395: \nL396: We utilize the Webis Native Ads 2024 datasetcite63†Schmidt et al. (2024) , consisting of user queries, system responses, and binary labels. The preprocessing pipeline:\nL397: \nL398:   * •\nL399: \nL400: Loads JSONL splits and extracts `responseText` and `label`.\nL401: \nL402:   * •\nL403: \nL404: Performs minimal normalization: original casing and whitespace are preserved to retain subtle cues.\nL405: \nL406:   * •\nL407: Tokenizes with the MPNet tokenizer from all-mpnet-base-v2, applying dynamic padding and truncation to 512 tokens to preserve semantic context.\nL408: \nL409:   * •\nL410: \nL411: Constructs training examples using sentence-transformers.InputExample with single-sentence input (`responseText` only) and its binary `label`.\nL412: \nL413:   * •\nL414: \nL415: No aggressive cleaning (e.g., stopword removal) is applied, to maintain advertisement cues’ integrity.\nL416: ##### Model Architecture and Justification:\nL417: \nL418: We fine-tune a CrossEncoder built on sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2cite75†Reimers and Gurevych (2019) , which includes 12 transformer layers ( 110M parameters) and enables full input sequence encoding for token-to-token interaction—crucial for detecting subtle promotional language.\nL419: MPNet, the model’s backbone, integrates masked language modeling (MLM) from BERT and permuted language modeling (PLM) from XLNet, while retaining full positional encoding. Trained on over 160 GB of text and fine-tuned on benchmarks like GLUE and SQuAD, MPNet outperforms BERT, XLNet, and RoBERTa by 4.8, 3.4, and 1.5 points respectively on GLUE dev sets under equivalent settings cite61†Song et al. (2020) ; cite73†Liu et al. (2019) ; cite76†Yang et al. (2020) ; cite72†Devlin et al. (2019) .\nL420: It also shows consistent improvements in SQuAD and other downstream tasks cite77†Rajpurkar et al. (2016) ; cite61†Song et al. (2020) .\nL421: This superior semantic fidelity makes MPNet ideal for high-precision native advertisement detection, outperforming lighter models (e.g., all-MiniLM-L6-v2) at a manageable computational cost cite74†Wang et al. (2020) ; cite75†Reimers and Gurevych (2019) . The model is adapted for binary classification by setting `num_labels=1` and applying a sigmoid activation to the logit output.\nL422: ##### Model Building and Training:\nL423: \nL424: We train the CrossEncoder using binary cross-entropy loss:\nL425: \nL426:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{BCE}}=-\\bigl[y\\cdot\\log\\hat{y}+(1-y)\\cdot\\log(1-\\hat{y})\\bigr]$$  |  | (3)\nL427: \nL428: where $y\\in\\{0,1\\}$ is the ground-truth label, and $\\hat{y}\\in(0,1)$ the predicted probability. Optimization uses AdamW with a linear warmup schedule.\nL429: Hyperparameter  | Value  | Rationale\nL430: Batch Size  | 16  | Efficient GPU usage without overfitting\nL431: Epochs  | 3  | Stable convergence with low variance\nL432: Learning Rate  | $2\\times 10^{-5}$  | Conservative steps for CrossEncoder\nL433: Warmup Steps  | 100  | Smooth gradient ramp-up\nL434: Weight Decay  | 0.01  | Regularization to avoid overfitting\nL435: Max Sequence Length  | 512 tokens  | Covers typical response length\nL436: #### 3.2.2 Approach 2\nL437: \nL438: ##### Task Formulation:\nL439: \nL440: The task is cast as a binary classification problem. Given a system-generated response and its corresponding query, the goal is to determine whether the response contains an advertisement. The desired output is a single label:\nL441: \nL442:   * •\nL443: \nL444: 1 if the response is promotional in nature,\nL445: \nL446:   * •\nL447: \nL448: 0 otherwise.\nL449: \nL450: To fully utilize the model’s instruction-following capabilities, we reformulate each data point as a natural language prompt.\nL451: ##### Input Construction and Preprocessing:\nL452: \nL453: The Touché-2024 dataset is used as the source corpus. Original .jsonl files are converted to .json using a custom utility for seamless integration with pandas. For each instance , the ”query”, ”response” and ”label is extracted and converted into the following prompt format:\nL454: This format enables the transformer model to better contextualize the classification task by explicitly posing it as an instruction. Tokenization is carried out using the DeBERTa tokenizer with truncation at 512 tokens (model max input length), padding to handle batch inputs, automatic generation of input-ids and attention-mask for training.\nL455: ##### Model Architecture and Justification:\nL456: \nL457: We fine-tune the microsoft/deberta-v3-base transformer with a binary classification head.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10594v1)\nciteturn29776view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view3\",\"lineno\":55}); Total lines: 239\nL45: Operational barriers such as implementation costs and shortages of AI skilled professionals constrain effective deployment and oversight. From a security perspective, digital transformation and AI systems also introduce risks of cyberattacks, data breaches, and adversarial manipulation, which can threaten business continuity [cite40†18 ]. Furthermore, weak governance structures, limited regulatory awareness, and absence of clear accountability mechanisms hinder SMEs from embedding responsible AI practices.\nL46: Collectively, these risks not only endanger customer trust and compliance but also expose SMEs to reputational damage, financial losses, and reduced competitiveness in the evolving digital economy.\nL47: cite41†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Typically involved technologies such as machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL) and large language models (LLMs) in the broad area of artificial intelligence (AI).\nL48: \nL49: cite42†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: An illustration of the key aspects - Trust (T), Ethics (E) and AI Modeling (AM) of SME-TEAM.\nL50: \nL51: cite43†Image: Refer to caption L52: Figure 3: A motivational scenario from the perspective of real-world applicability of SME-TEAM.\nL53: To overcome these challenges, SMEs must embed trust and ethical principles at the core of their AI adoption strategies. By prioritizing transparency, explainability, accountability, and secure-by-design approaches in SMEs, technological innovation aligns with societal values and regulatory expectations. Explainable AI mechanisms [cite44†19 , cite45†20 , cite46†21 ] allow stakeholders to understand decision-making processes, strengthening trust among customers, employees, and regulators.\nL54: Ethical design practices, combined with human oversight, help mitigate bias, ensure equitable outcomes and align AI use with societal values. Importantly, sector-specific tailoring such as bias mitigation in financial services, or transparent consent mechanisms in healthcare, ensures that ethical safeguards align with contextual needs. By adopting secure-by-design architectures and maintaining robust governance strategy, SMEs can balance innovation with responsibility.\nL55: Ultimately, trust and ethics shift AI adoption from being a potential risk to becoming a strategic driver of competitiveness, resilience, and sustainable innovation in SMEs.\nL56: Building on these insights, this paper contributes to both theory and practice by addressing the often-overlooked intersection of AI adoption, trust, and ethics within the SME context. First, it advances theoretical understanding by reframing trust and ethics not as constraints but as enablers of competitiveness, resilience, and innovation in digitally transforming SMEs.\nL57: Second, it introduces a structured, multi-phased framework “SME-TEAM\", Trust, Ethics and AI Modeling for SMEs (as shown in Figure cite47†2 ) that bridges high-level ethical principles with actionable practices through its four pillars: Data, Algorithms, Human Oversight, and Model Architecture.\nL58: Third, it extends research implications by demonstrating the framework’s potentiality and applicability across SME sectors, encouraging future studies to examine contextual challenges and strategies for sustainable digital transformation. Collectively, these contributions provide both an academic roadmap and practical guidance for SMEs, policymakers, and technology providers seeking to harness AI securely and responsibly.\nL59: ## 2 Digital Transformation and AI in SMEs: Current Practice\nL60: ### 2.1 Digital Transformation of SMEs\nL61: Digital transformation [cite48†22 , cite49†23 , cite50†24 , cite51†25 , cite52†26 ] has become a defining feature of the modern SME landscape, enabling smaller enterprises to modernize operations, improve efficiency, and strengthen competitiveness in digital markets. This shift is increasingly driven by the integration of AI into both operational workflows and strategic decision-making, positioning it as a catalyst for SME growth, innovation, and long-term competitiveness [cite53†27 ].\nL62: Current adoption trends focus on automating repetitive tasks, enhancing customer engagement through chatbots and recommendation systems, and applying data analytics for informed decision-making. For example, Yuwono et al. [cite54†28 ] highlight the use of ICT across diverse SME sectors such as manufacturing, agriculture, and mining; Indiani et al. [cite55†29 ] examine how digital transformation unlocks e-commerce potential; Kohli et al.\nL63: [cite48†22 ] emphasize its role in strengthening supply chain management; El et al. [cite34†12 ] explore applications in sustainable agriculture; and Vukovic et al. [cite36†14 ] along with Lu et al. [cite37†15 ] focus on financial services. Similarly, Treasure et al. [cite33†11 ] highlight healthcare applications, Graziosi et al. [cite32†10 ] discuss sustainable additive manufacturing, Da et al. [cite51†25 ] analyze manufacturing competitiveness, and De et al. [cite56†30 ] examine the role of digital twin technology in SMEs.\nL64: While these practices demonstrate the potential of AI and digital technologies to enhance efficiency and competitiveness, they also reveal the uneven readiness of SMEs, largely due to gaps in secure, ethical, and responsible AI adoption.\nL65: ### 2.2 Revolution of Today’s AI and LLMs\nL66: AI has rapidly progressed from a theoretical concept to a transformative force reshaping industries, business operations, and decision-making. Over the past decade, breakthroughs in machine learning, data analytics, natural language processing, and deep learning [cite57†31 , cite38†16 , cite26†5 ] have enabled AI systems to process large datasets, detect intricate patterns, and deliver predictive insights with remarkable accuracy.\nL67: These advancements not only enhance productivity but also empower organizations to pursue data-driven strategies that boost adaptability and competitiveness. A number of studies, including Wei et al. [cite27†6 ], Arroyabe et al. [cite58†32 ], Carayannis et al. [cite59†33 ], and Mantri et al. [cite60†34 ], underscore the crucial role of AI adoption in improving SME performance, innovation, and long-term competitiveness.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbduct, Act, Predict:Scaffolding Causal Inference for Automated Failure Attribution in Multi-Agent Systems (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10401v1)\nciteturn29776view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view4\",\"lineno\":96}); Total lines: 240\nL90: Mathematically, A2P Scaffolding approximates the estimation of a counterfactual outcome $Z(\\mathcal{I}_{(i,t)}(\\tau))$ for an intervention at step $t$. We formalize the failure attribution task within Pearl’s SCM framework where a trajectory $\\tau$ is generated by structural equations with states evolving as $s_{t+1}=f(s_{t},a_{t},\\epsilon_{t})$, where $\\epsilon_{t}$ represents unobserved exogenous variables (e.g., agent’s internal knowledge state).\nL91: The final outcome $Z(\\tau)$ is a function of the full trajectory. Our objective is to find the earliest pair $(i^{*},t^{*})=\\arg\\min_{(i,t)}t$ such that the LLM’s guided simulation predicts $Z(\\mathcal{I}_{(i,t)}(\\tau))=0$ (success). The A2P framework guides the LLM through three approximations:\nL92:  | Abduction:  | $\\displaystyle\\epsilon_{t}\\leftarrow\\arg\\max_{\\epsilon}P(\\epsilon|s_{0:t},a_{t},Z(\\tau)=1)$  |  | (1)\nL93:  | Action:  | $\\displaystyle do(a_{t}\\leftarrow a_{t}^{*})$  |  | (2)\nL94:  | Prediction:  | $\\displaystyle Z(\\tau^{*})=g(s_{0},\\ldots,s_{t},s_{t+1}^{*},\\ldots)\\text{ where }s_{t+1}^{*}=f(s_{t},a_{t}^{*},\\epsilon_{t})$  |  | (3)\nL95: This entire three-step process is executed for each potential error the model considers, and it ultimately outputs the earliest agent-step pair that satisfies this causal chain. To support this fine-grained temporal reasoning, our method incorporates a critical structural component: Contextual Step Numbering. Before being passed to the model, the entire conversation log is pre-processed to prefix each turn with an explicit, formatted identifier like Step {idx} - Agent_Name:.\nL96: Our ablation experiments conclusively demonstrate that these structural anchors are not merely a minor enhancement but are absolutely essential, preventing a catastrophic drop in step-level accuracy by providing the model with unambiguous reference points to trace causal dependencies through the dialogue.\nL97: The implementation is seamlessly integrated into the existing codebase through a command-line flag --causal_reasoning that activates the construct_causal_prompt function within the all_at_once and all_at_once_async methods. This design ensures full backward compatibility while making our advanced causal analysis easily accessible.\nL98: The computational overhead is minimal, consisting of a 25% increase in processing time and token count per sample—a modest cost for the 2.85$\\times$ improvement in accuracy achieved by our method.\nL99: Having established the theoretical foundation and implementation details of A2P Scaffolding, we proceed to describe our comprehensive experimental methodology designed to rigorously evaluate the framework’s effectiveness across diverse multi-agent system configurations and failure scenarios.\nL100: cite52†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Overview of the A2P Scaffolding framework. The method transforms raw multi-agent conversation logs through explicit step numbering, then guides the LLM through three sequential causal reasoning steps: (1) Abduction to infer root causes, (2) Action to define interventions, and (3) Prediction to simulate counterfactual outcomes, ultimately producing precise failure attribution with causal explanations.\nL101: ## 4 Experimental Setup\nL102: All experiments were conducted on the Who&When benchmark (cite22†Zhang et al., 2025 ), a comprehensive dataset specifically designed for automated failure attribution in multi-agent systems. The benchmark comprises two distinct subsets that provide complementary perspectives on system complexity: Algorithm-Generated (126 samples) and Hand-Crafted (58 samples), totaling 184 distinct failure attribution tasks.\nL103: The Algorithm-Generated subset contains failure logs from systems automatically constructed using the CaptainAgent algorithm from the AG2 library, where each system is tailored to specific queries from the GAIA (cite53†Mialon et al., 2023 ) and AssistantBench (cite54†Yoran et al., 2024 ) validation sets. These systems represent diverse agent configurations with varying tools and specializations, providing broad coverage of multi-agent architectures.\nL104: The Hand-Crafted subset features failure logs from Magnetic-One (cite21†Fourney et al., 2024 ), a mature, carefully engineered multi-agent system comprising five specialized agents designed for web browsing, file navigation, and complex task orchestration. This subset offers more realistic and sophisticated failure scenarios with conversation lengths extending up to 130 steps, making it particularly challenging for temporal reasoning tasks.\nL105: Our method, A2P Scaffolding, was implemented by modifying the baseline all_at_once approach to incorporate our structured causal reasoning prompt, activated via a --causal_reasoning command-line flag. We used the gpt-oss-120b model accessed via a local API endpoint to ensure consistent experimental conditions across all methods. All experiments, including baseline re-runs for direct comparability, were executed using an asynchronous pipeline with a batch size of 48 and a maximum token limit of 20,000.\nL106: This configuration enables efficient processing while maintaining the quality of generated responses. The experimental infrastructure was deployed on NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3 GPUs running on Linux 5.14.0-427.13.1.el9_4.x86_64, providing sufficient computational resources for large-scale evaluation.\nL107: Performance evaluation employs two primary metrics that capture different aspects of attribution accuracy. Agent-Level Accuracy measures the percentage of correctly predicted failure-responsible agents, representing the fundamental requirement for identifying which component of the multi-agent system caused the failure. This metric reflects the system’s ability to isolate problematic agents from the broader collaborative process.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnderstanding Outer Optimizers in Local SGD: Learning Rates, Momentum, and Acceleration (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10439v1)\nciteturn29776view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view5\",\"lineno\":144}); Total lines: 1234\nL132: ###### Assumption 3.1.\nL133: \nL134: The function $f$ is differentiable, convex, has $L$-Lipschitz gradients, and has a minimizer $x_{\\ast}$.\nL135: \nL136: We suppose that we can access a stochastic first-order oracle that given a point $x$ returns a gradient $g(x)$ that satisfies the following assumption.\nL137: ###### Assumption 3.2.\nL138: \nL139: Given a point $x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$, the stochastic gradients $g(x)\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}$ are (a) unbiased in expectation $\\mathbb{E}\\left[g(x)\\right]=\\nabla f(x)$, and (b) has variance bounded as $\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\left\\lVert g(x)-\\nabla f(x)\\right\\rVert^{2}\\right]\\leq\\sigma^{2}$, where $\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\cdot\\right]$ denotes the expectation operator.\nL140: Our setting is distributed, but with identically distributed data: there are $M$ different nodes, but they all sample stochastic gradients from the same data distribution in an i.i.d. (independent and identically distributed) manner. We denote the inner product between two vectors $a$ and $b$ by $\\left\\langle a,b\\right\\rangle$ and by $\\left\\lVert\\cdot\\right\\rVert$ the corresponding Euclidean norm. For the purpose of theoretical analysis, can write Generalized Local SGD succinctly as\nL141:  | $\\displaystyle\\vskip-2.84526pty_{m,r,0}$  | $\\displaystyle=x_{r},\\qquad g_{m,r,h}=\\text{Stochastic gradient of $y_{m,r,h}$}$  |\nL142:  | $\\displaystyle y_{m,r,h+1}$  | $\\displaystyle=y_{m,r,h}-\\eta g_{m,r,h},\\text{ for }m=1,\\ldots,M\\text{ in parallel and }h=0,1,\\ldots,H-1\\text{ in sequence. }$  |\nL143:  | $\\displaystyle x_{r+1}$  | $\\displaystyle=x_{r}-\\gamma\\eta\\sum_{h=0}^{H-1}\\frac{1}{M}\\sum_{m=1}^{M}g_{m,r,h}.$  |  | (GEN-LOC-SGD)\nL144: To simplify our analysis, we follow (cite43†Stich, 2019 ) and define the virtual sequences\nL145: \nL146:  | $\\displaystyle\\vskip-2.84526pt\\begin{split}y_{r,h}\\overset{\\text{def}}{=}\\frac{1}{M}\\sum_{m=1}^{M}y_{m,r,h},\\qquad\\qquad g_{r,h}\\overset{\\text{def}}{=}\\frac{1}{M}\\sum_{m=1}^{M}g_{m,r,h}\\\\\nL147: \\overline{g}_{m,r,h}\\overset{\\text{def}}{=}\\mathbb{E}_{r,h-1}\\left[g_{m,r,h}\\right]=\\nabla f(y_{m,r,h}),\\qquad\\qquad\\overline{g}_{r,h}\\overset{\\text{def}}{=}\\mathbb{E}_{r,h-1}\\left[g_{r,h}\\right].\\end{split}$  |  | (1)\nL148: #### 3.2 Main convergence result\nL149: \nL150: Recall that we consider Algorithm cite40†1 the particular case when $\\mathrm{LocalUpdate}(y,g)=y-\\eta g$ and $\\mathrm{OuterUpdate}(x,\\Delta)=x-\\gamma\\Delta$.\nL151: Existing results on the convergence of Gen. Local SGD. When the outer stepsize $\\gamma=1$, the convergence of (cite85†GEN-LOC-SGD ) is very well understood, with tightly matching upper and lower bounds (cite44†Khaled et al., 2020 ; cite45†Woodworth et al., 2020a ; cite47†Glasgow et al., 2022 ). In particular, the best rate for the algorithm is\nL152:  | $$\\!\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[\\!f\\!\\left(\\!\\frac{1}{RH}\\!\\sum_{r=0}^{R-1}\\!\\sum_{h=0}^{H-1}\\!y_{r,h}\\!\\right)\\!\\right]\\!-f(x_{\\ast})\\leq\\mathcal{O}\\!\\left(\\!\\frac{L{\\left\\lVert x_{0}\\!-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}^{2}}{RH}+\\frac{\\sigma\\left\\lVert x_{0}\\!-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}{\\sqrt{MRH}}+\\frac{L^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\sigma^{\\frac{2}{3}}\\left\\lVert x_{0}\\!-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert^{\\frac{4}{3}}}{H^{\\frac{1}{3}}R^{\\frac{2}{3}}}\\!\\right).$$  |  | (2)\nL153: The first two terms in the above convergence guarantee show that increasing the number of local steps has the same effect as increasing the number of communication rounds $R$, and are identical to the convergence guarantee of doing $RH$ steps of SGD with minibatch size $M$. Local SGD differs from ordinary minibatch SGD in the last term, which shows different scaling between $H$ and $R$, where increasing $R$ helps more than increasing $H$.\nL154: This is because increasing $H$ incurrs additional client drift that slows down the convergence of the algorithm in the presence of stochastic gradient noise. When the outer stepsize $\\gamma$ is allowed to vary, the convergence of the algorithm is less clear. cite58†Karimireddy et al. (2020) gives the following convergence rate in the absence of data heterogeneity,\nL155:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[f\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\sum_{r=0}^{R-1}x_{r}\\right)\\right]-f(x_{\\ast})\\leq\\mathcal{O}\\left(\\frac{L{\\left\\lVert x_{0}-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}^{2}}{R}+\\frac{\\sigma\\left\\lVert x_{0}-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}{\\sqrt{MR}}\\right),$  |\nL156: for specially chosen $\\eta$ and $\\gamma$ pairs. This rate matches that of Minibatch SGD, but does not recover the convergence rate of vanilla Local SGD given by cite86†Equation 2 . cite50†Jhunjhunwala et al. (2023) also give a guarantee for Generalized Local SGD with a specific outer learning rate that is always at least $1$ and that depends on the heterogeneity of the iterates across the different clients.\nL157: Since the analysis is conducted in the heterogeneous setting, the local stepsize required to scale with $1/H$. A guarantee that applies to any outer learning rate in the nonconvex, heterogeneous setting given by (cite51†Sun et al., 2024 ).\nL158: The limiting factor in existing analysis is that we are forced to choose the local stepsize $\\eta$ to scale as $\\frac{1}{LH}$, whereas to obtain cite86†Equation 2 we sometimes need to choose $\\eta$ to be much larger, on the order of $\\frac{1}{L}$. If we aim to accurately characterize the convergence of (cite85†GEN-LOC-SGD ), our analysis has to encompass both large and small local stepsizes $\\eta$.\nL159: \nL160: New analysis. We now present our main convergence theorem for (cite85†GEN-LOC-SGD ).\nL161: ###### Theorem 3.3.\nL162: \nL163: Suppose that cite87†Assumptions 3.1 and cite88†3.2 hold. Then the iterates generated by Generalized Local SGD run with local stepsize $\\eta>0$ and outer stepsize $\\gamma>0$ for $R$ communication rounds and with $H$ local steps per round satisfy,\n"
